sweat plays a huge role in the world of  dune the novel takes place mostly on the  planet arrakis a dangerously hot planet  where inhabitants must recycle their own  sweat to survive but despite sweat's  life-saving role the film adaptation  rarely shows anybody sweating at all and  when it does show sweat it's  inconsistent of course this is probably  not an accident or an oversight it's an  intentional choice by the filmmakers and  this use of sweat in dune not only shows  the importance of sweat in movies as a  whole but it also reveals why a movie  like dune needed more of it so let's  take a look at some of our favorite  movie characters and ask them a question  why are you so sweaty  [Music]  at first glance sweat doesn't carry the  same emotional weight as more direct  emotional cues like seeing blood or  watching someone cry sweat doesn't  signify death or sadness it's a  seemingly straightforward biological  response to heat or stress but like  blood and tears sweat in movies is  carefully choreographed the sweat we see  in movies is often controlled not by the  actor's sweat glands but by the makeup  department it's not like sylvester  stallone can maintain that insane level  of sweat for an entire day of shooting  between takes makeup artist leonard  engelman sprayed him down with a water  glycerin mixture that mimics the  viscosity of sweat making stallone look  hard at work thanks to movie magic  actors who look sweaty and exhausted on  screen could actually feel quite  comfortable on set but that's not always  the case makeup artists also have to  deal with the opposite keeping  uncomfortable sweaty actors looking dry  whether it's thick costumes makeup  prosthetics or powerful lighting  equipment film sets tend to run on the  sweaty side like when the actors in it's  a wonderful life had to wear winter  coats while filming on a sound stage in  90 degree weather the dojo scene in the  matrix required intense lights so the  camera could capture slow motion which  meant the makeup team had to pat down  the actors in between takes the reason  to remove sweat is obvious for it's a  wonderful life it would look strange for  actors to look hot in the winter but in  the matrix the lack of sweat does more  by explaining the world of the matrix  morpheus shouldn't sweat in the dojo  because he knows how to bend the laws of  physics in the matrix and since neo  hasn't learned that yet he still sweats  so when we do see morpheus sweat during  his interrogation we can tell he's no  longer in control so sweat can work as a  sort of visual shorthand showing who's  under pressure and who isn't don't say  it don't you say left come on and this  phenomenon reveals what makes sweat so  important in movies sweat isn't just a  biological response to heat it's also a  biological response to stress something  wrong  patrick  you're sweating so directors use sweat  in this way to give us insight into  certain characters even within the same  exact room the emotional characters can  sweat a lot while the cool analytical  ones don't sweat at all don't you ever  sweat  no i don't you may not notice it  consciously but our lizard brains  definitely associate sweat with stress  remember the opening shot of the first  teaser trailer for the force awakens  have you felt it  the narration is right you can feel it  sweat is crucial to this moment because  it signals that finn is in the middle of  a chase and it adds to the mystery of  what's off screen and where all the  sounds are coming from compare that to  paul in dune who looks like he's just  getting started  the movie dune shows just how much  creative liberty makeup departments have  when it comes to sweat many characters  don't sweat at all or they sweat  inconsistently take this scene where one  character sweats but paul is bone dry  i'm sure most people watching the movie  didn't notice this inconsistency but  this choice does have an emotional  effect on the viewer by making paul the  dry one in this scene he feels more calm  royal and unaffected by the toxic  environment filmmakers often reserve  sweat for those actively working which  has the added effect of separating the  pampered elite from the ones performing  the labor in this way sweat can  emphasize class divides showing who  suffers and who gets to stay dry in blue  collar jerry starts off as a sweaty auto  worker but by the end he is transformed  into a white collar worker with a clean  brow so sweat can accomplish much more  than just showing the temperature and  it's a wonderful life james stewart's  character actually sweats a lot even  during a winter snowstorm stewart  probably looked like that because the  set was nearly 100 degrees but it  doesn't feel unrealistic on the contrary  by having him sweat in the middle of  winter the audience feels his emotional  anguish that much more some filmmakers  go one step further and make their  characters sweatier than humanly  possible this exaggeration usually leads  to comedy but it can also work in  dramatic movies in raging bull the  makeup team had to cover robert de niro  in vaseline so that sweat would fly off  his face when he moved and that effect  combined with the animal sound effects  and disorienting camera work creates a  boxing ring full of violence and chaos  but perhaps the greatest practitioner of  sweaty characters comes from the  director sergio leone take the nine  minute opening credits in his spaghetti  western once upon a time in the west  which consists of three silent  gunslingers sitting around waiting for  the train a scene so uneventful should  feel incredibly boring so how did sergio  leone make a bunch of people doing  nothing feel so electric part of the  magic comes from the characters menacing  introductions and the initial mystery of  why they appeared at the station but i  think the true reason why leone could  hold onto these characters for so long  is simply because they're also irritated  one has water drip on his head one  spends way too long dealing with a fly  and the other sits around looking bored  all while a windmill creaks in the  background not much happens physically  but a lot happens emotionally they  aren't moving but they are sweating so  you can feel their internal intensity  long before they fire their guns when  you really think about it the majority  of a shootout in a western movie is just  two guys staring each other down the  characters aren't actually doing  anything but we can feel the stakes  beneath that motionless surface in these  moments each beat of sweat matters as  much as the sound design or the camera  or the film score because sweat betrays  the true intensity within the stillness  but the characters in sergio leone films  tend to sweat not just during climactic  moments but throughout the entire movie  sweat creates a lingering reminder of  the constant uncomfortable heap and of  the never-ending harshness of their  world other movies like dog day  afternoon and do the right thing  similarly use constant sweat to show  pent-up tension in their respective  brooklyn neighborhoods but dune chooses  not to go this route paul doesn't sweat  when it's hot he only sweats when he's  undergoing mental anguish like during  the pain test scene or when he  hallucinates his sweating inside the  tent gives the scene a claustrophobic  feel and the way his sweat combines with  his tears creates an emotional cocktail  that really sells the moment so the dune  makeup department reserves sweat for the  internal climactic moments but that  choice comes at the expense of not  really getting a sense for the external  heat of the planet and i think that's a  shame because going the route of the  spaghetti western would have given the  viewer a constant reminder of the  inhospitable nature of the planet  arrakis  well when i came home from school my  head started to get really hot  at the end of the day sweat is one of  the great human equalizers everybody  does it and it's not something a person  has control over so when the makeup  department makes a decision on sweat  they're essentially choosing to add or  remove humanity from a character or from  a setting so when dune doesn't use sweat  often it subtly makes house atreides  feel invincible when in reality the  house had trouble adjusting to the harsh  environment of arrakis we don't get to  feel that tension as strongly with such  dry looking characters in a world where  sweat literally keeps you alive i think  the absence of sweat drains some life  from the story and in a cinematic  landscape increasingly propped up by cgi  and polished big budget experiences  there's more need than ever for the  delightfully human imperfection that is  sweat because we don't go to the movies  just to see an external conflict we also  go to see an internal one so the next  time you watch a great movie remember  that it took three things to make it  blood  [Music]  as always thank you to my patreon  supporters who got to see the video  early and some even got to help out with  the script thanks for watching
 

